{"acceptance_length": 8.057692307692308, "acceptance_lengths": [1, 3, 3, 8, 6, 4, 6, 9, 2, 16, 9, 2, 3, 2, 14, 15, 16, 16, 12, 5, 9, 1, 16, 16, 16, 16, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3, 2, 5, 1, 1, 16, 16, 16, 16, 5, 7, 14, 16, 10, 16, 16, 11, 3, 3, 3, 1, 4], "accepted_draft_lengths": [0, 2, 2, 7, 5, 3, 5, 8, 1, 15, 8, 1, 2, 1, 13, 14, 15, 15, 11, 4, 8, 0, 15, 15, 15, 15, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 1, 4, 0, 0, 15, 15, 15, 15, 4, 6, 13, 15, 9, 15, 15, 10, 2, 2, 2, 0, 3], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 23421981696, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 23421971968, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "To find the **nth Jacobsthal number**, we can use the recurrence relation:\n\n$$\nJ(n) = J(n-1) + 2 \\cdot J(n-2)\n$$\n\nWith the base cases:\n- $ J(0) = 0 $\n- $ J(1) = 1 $\n\nWe can implement this using a simple iterative approach.\n\nHere's the Python function:\n\n```python\ndef jacobsthal_num(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, b + 2 * a\n    return b\n```\n\n### Test the function:\n\n```python\nassert jacobsthal_num(5) == 11\nassert jacobsthal_num(2) == 1\nassert jacobsthal_num(4) == 5\nassert jacobsthal_num(13) == 2731\n```\n\nAll assertions will pass.\n\n### Explanation of the sequence:\nThe Jacobsthal numbers are:\n- $ J(0) = 0 $\n- $ J(1) = 1 $\n- $ J(2) = 1 $\n- $ J(3) = 3 $\n- $ J(4) = 5 $\n- $ J(5) = 11 $\n- $ J(6) = 21 $\n- $ J(7) = 43 $\n- $ J(8) = 85 $\n- $ J(9) = 171 $\n- $ J(10) = 341 $\n- $ J(11) = 683 $\n- $ J(12) = 1365 $\n- $ J(13) = 2731 $\n\n\u2705 The function is correct and passes all the given tests.", "decode_seconds": 1.922365861013532, "draft_calls": 52, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 170, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "ece1269540becf19b73b75b0ca48a5dfd6178ffe372c6606870efbbab8ae580a", "method": "relay_base", "output_hash": "d5d52f62cb22d445839e95a5793db544632b4b8caed491ca1d58003dcf0a04b5", "output_tokens": 418, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 23620825088, "peak_rese