{"acceptance_length": 5.277777777777778, "acceptance_lengths": [3, 1, 1, 2, 7, 5, 1, 5, 3, 5, 2, 1, 6, 9, 6, 2, 6, 6, 3, 2, 4, 5, 3, 3, 8, 7, 4, 1, 3, 2, 8, 6, 16, 6, 16, 3, 2, 12, 5, 6, 5, 3, 12, 5, 8, 12, 6, 6, 16, 7, 3, 1, 1, 4], "accepted_draft_lengths": [2, 0, 0, 1, 6, 4, 0, 4, 2, 4, 1, 0, 5, 8, 5, 1, 5, 5, 2, 1, 3, 4, 2, 2, 7, 6, 3, 0, 2, 1, 7, 5, 15, 5, 15, 2, 1, 11, 4, 5, 4, 2, 11, 4, 7, 11, 5, 5, 15, 6, 2, 0, 0, 3], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 23421983232, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 23421972480, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "You can solve this by iterating through the list of tuples and extracting the last element of each tuple. In Python, you can access the last element of a tuple using negative indexing (`-1`).\n\nHere's the function:\n\n```python\ndef rear_extract(tuples_list):\n    return [t[-1] for t in tuples_list]\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- `t[-1]` gets the last element of each tuple `t`.\n- The list comprehension `[t[-1] for t in tuples_list]` creates a new list containing these last elements.\n\n### Test Cases:\n```python\nassert rear_extract([(1, 'Rash', 21), (2, 'Varsha', 20), (3, 'Kil', 19)]) == [21, 20, 19]\nassert rear_extract([(1, 'Sai', 36), (2, 'Ayesha', 25), (3, 'Salman', 45)]) == [36, 25, 45]\nassert rear_extract([(1, 'Sudeep', 14), (2, 'Vandana', 36), (3, 'Dawood', 56)]) == [14, 36, 56]\n```\n\nAll assertions will pass with this implementation.", "decode_seconds": 1.99171500839293, "draft_calls": 54, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 248, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "1731be3c1200b11f669250c83273ab1eb2ec82552d7e1ee06d5b99f852706a1c", "method": "relay_one", "output_hash": "ebdb87382c38bf45840444ce1d6cf0cc800ca8e29d8ac7501d80f80a7c7db27c", "output_tokens": 284, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 23651268096, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 23819452416, "problem_id": "Mbpp/272", "profile_regions_ms": {"draft": 338.7571520805359, "prefill_full_target": 36.83059310913086, "prefill_relay": 0.1005759984254837, "relay": 10.2756